{/*
  The quiet section — 4 on the intensity budget, and deliberately the slowest
  thing on the page. Contrast is the point: after the work plates, almost
  nothing here moves except one masked phrase and a ghost word drifting at a
  fifth of scroll speed.

  This is the only place on the page that speaks in the first person about the
  person rather than about the work. It deliberately does NOT list the stacks:
  Capabilities below is generated from what actually appears in a shipped
  project, and a hand-written copy of the same lists here would be a second
  version of the truth, free to drift from the first. The third paragraph
  hands over to it instead.
*/}
<section class="about chapter shell" id="about" data-chapter>
  {/* DRIFT. The one word the section is actually about. */}
  {/* --ghost-len is the character count: the ghost sizes itself so the whole
      word fits the sheet at any viewport, instead of being cut at both edges
      by a fixed vw. See `.ghost` in motion.css. */}
  <span
    class="ghost"
    data-fam="drift"
    data-rate="0.18"
    style="--ghost-len: 10"
    aria-hidden="true"
  >Dependable</span>

  <div class="about__grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h2 class="about__statement" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
        I'm Sugam Pokharel, an 18-year-old web developer building products that
        are <em>practical, fast and dependable.</em>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="about__copy">
      <p>
        I work across the whole development process — from the interfaces people
        interact with, to the backend systems, integrations and deployment setup
        that keep everything running once it is live.
      </p>
      <p>
        That means clean, responsive frontends; backends and databases that stay
        reliable beyond the UI; and the last mile most side projects never reach
        — payments, API integrations, hosting and deployment.
      </p>
      <p>
        What I actually reach for on each of those is listed below, alongside a
        proof point from something I have shipped. The tools support the claim;
        they are not the claim by themselves.
      </p>
      <p>
        I am constantly learning and improving by building real projects and
        exploring new technologies, and I am always open to interesting work,
        collaborations, and the chance to build something useful.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
